+    /// Tier 4 — **case split**. The e-graph cannot reason by cases: an `ite`
+    /// whose condition is an unconstrained boolean stays opaque, so a fact that
+    /// holds in *both* branches is never concluded. That is exactly the shape a
+    /// Rust `match` lowers to — `discr(e)` is `ite(is_C1(e), cons(1), cons(0))`,
+    /// and the `otherwise` arm's `assert false` is refutable only by knowing the
+    /// discriminant is one of its finitely many values.
+    ///
+    /// So: pick an `ite` condition `c` reachable from the goal or the path
+    /// condition, and prove the goal twice — once assuming `c`, once assuming
+    /// `¬c`. If both hold, the goal holds.
+    ///
+    /// Searched by **iterative deepening**, not depth-first. The candidate
+    /// ranking below is a heuristic and gets the order wrong often enough that a
+    /// depth-first search burns its whole budget recursing under a bad first
+    /// pick. Sweeping every candidate at depth 1 before any at depth 2 means the
+    /// overwhelming majority of goals — which need exactly one split — cost
+    /// `2·|candidates|` saturations and never recurse, and a wrong guess costs
+    /// one level, not a subtree. `budget` caps total probe saturations so a goal
+    /// that is simply not provable this way degrades gracefully instead of
+    /// exploding.
+    ///
+    /// Only reached when tier 3 has already failed, so the common path pays
+    /// nothing.
+    fn split_prove(
